  - [3mNotification permission denied or dismissed[0m — degrade silently to the in-app inbox and never re-prompt. The inbox is
    the baseline that always works; the service worker is an enhancement on top.
  - [3m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m unavailable or full[0m (private browsing, quota) — the store surfaces a typed error and the app shows a
    persistent banner and goes read-only. The failure mode to avoid is accepting writes that silently vanish.
  - [3mCorrupt stored records[0m — validate on read; quarantine records that don't parse rather than wiping the store. A parse
    bug shouldn't cost you your task list.

  Time is always injected, never read ambiently. Every due-date comparison takes [38;5;153mnow[39m as a parameter.

  [1mTesting[0m (Vitest, unit only):
  - Model predicates — boundary cases are the point: exactly at the due instant, lead-time edges, DST shifts, tasks with
    no due date.
  - Store — against an in-memory [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m fake, including the change-event contract, since two subscribers depend on
    it.
  - Scheduler — with a fake clock and a fake [38;5;153mNotifier[39m: fires once and only once, re-arms when a due date moves, catches
    up correctly on load.
  - The service worker stays untested by design, which is why it holds no logic.

  [1mScope split.[0m Sub-project 1: model, store, UI, tooling, and the change-event contract. Sub-project 2: scheduler, dedup
  records, inbox, service worker, lead-time setting.

  [1mExplicitly out[0m (say if any of these is actually a requirement): per-task reminders, daily digest, snooze, recurring
  tasks, tags/priority, sharing, auth, and notification history beyond the inbox.
